── linux ──────────────────────────────────────────────
PR #180  overall: potential_risk
Spec: Spec elevates MDCFG cascade-lock from 'should' to 'must' and clarifies entry priority override: locking a domain entry requires all predecessor domain entries also be locked, otherwise an attacker manipulates the unlocked predecessor's tail to inject or remove entries in the locked domain, defeating IOPMP isolation.
  [OK] arch/riscv/include/asm/csr.h:125-133  (none — preprocessor defines)
      These are pure #define constants reflecting the RISC-V privileged ISA's PMP CSR bit layout. PMP_L (0x80) and PMP_A_TOR (0x08) are architecturally correct. There is no executable logic to be wrong. The non-cascading-lock vulnerability is inherent in the ISA's choice to make PMP_L per-entry rather than transitive — this file merely names the bits as the ISA defines them.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/head.S:229-242  _start_kernel
      M-mode early boot configures PMP entry 0 (highest hardware priority) as NAPOT all-memory RWX WITHOUT setting PMP_L. This creates an unlocked highest-priority entry that matches every physical access first. Currently safe because (a) the kernel IS M-mode — there is no higher-privilege attacker, and (b) no locked PMP entries exist yet to bypass. However, the code depends on the implicit invariant that any later PMP configuration (e.g. locking lower-priority entries for S-mode protection) will correctly lock or remove this entry 0. If this entry is left unlocked while lower-priority entries are locked, an attacker who gains M-mode code execution can rewrite entry 0 to override all locked entries — the exact cascading-lock bypass pattern. This code is the 'smoking gun' demonstrating zero awareness of cascading-lock requirements in the kernel.
  [RISK] drivers/iommu/riscv/iommu-bits.h:334-346  (none — preprocessor defines)
      Defines RISCV_IOMMU_DC_TC_V (Valid bit) and related translation-control fields for Device Context entries. A search of the entire iommu-bits.h reveals zero hardware lock bits — no ENTRYLCK, no LOCK field of any kind — for either Device Context entries or DDT entries. This is the structural analogue to PMP_L but entirely absent: no IOMMU table entry can ever be made hardware-immutable. The severity is potential_risk rather than bug_confirmed because this reflects a hardware architecture limitation, not a driver software defect. A future IOPMP specification mandating cascading MDCFGLCK would have no corresponding IOMMU hardware mechanism to mirror.
  [RISK] drivers/iommu/riscv/iommu.c:617-638  riscv_iommu_get_dc
      Non-leaf DDT entry population uses cmpxchg with only the Valid bit as guard. Correct for concurrent-kernel-thread safety, but once a non-leaf node is Valid, a racing write can atomically cmpxchg-replace it with a new PPN, redirecting the entire subtree of device contexts below. This is structurally identical to the IOPMP cascading-lock vulnerability: a parent/ancestor entry defines the boundary of all descendants, yet rewriting the parent has no cascading lock check. There is no software-level invariant enforcement (e.g. 'refuse to replace a non-leaf DDT entry that has valid descendants'). The code depends entirely on the implicit invariant that all IOMMU-table writers are trusted kernel code — no protection against a compromised or buggy peer writer.
  [OK] drivers/clk/thead/clk-th1520-ap.c:1250-1261  th1520_vo_gate_clks
      Defines IOPMP clock gates for the TH1520 SoC video-output subsystem. Six IOPMP clocks are registered: three PCLK (peripheral bus clocks for DPU, DPU1, GPU IOPMP instances) and three ACLK (AXI bus clocks, all marked CLK_IS_CRITICAL meaning they cannot be disabled at runtime). These are pure data declarations — register offset, bit position, parent clock, flags. No IOPMP policy, no entry configuration, no lock semantics. Their relevance is contextual: they confirm IOPMP hardware mediates DMA traffic from GPU and display processors (high-bandwidth, DMA-capable peripherals), which is exactly the attack surface where a non-cascading-lock bypass would let an attacker redirect DMA to protected memory.
  [OK] include/dt-bindings/clock/thead,th1520-clk-ap.h:121-126  (none — preprocessor defines)
      Integer constant definitions (CLK_IOPMP_VOSYS_DPU_PCLK=22 through CLK_IOPMP_GPU_ACLK=27) for device-tree clock binding. Pure #define macros with no executable logic. Paired evidence with the clock driver (location 5), confirming IOPMP hardware integration on TH1520 at the device-tree binding layer. The numeric values are sequential and consistent with the surrounding clock IDs. No security-relevant logic exists to be wrong.

