      This is a pure #include of <sbi/sbi_tlb.h>, which provides the declaration for __sbi_sfence_vma_all(). No relationship to IOPMP MDCFG lock semantics. The include is correct and carries no security implication relevant to the spec.
  [OK] lib/sbi/sbi_domain_context.c:139-141  switch_to_next_domain_context
      The __sbi_sfence_vma_all() fence after csr_swap(CSR_SATP, ...) is correct MMU synchronization, ensuring TLB translations match the new domain's address width (e.g. Sv39->Sv48). This code manages CPU-side MMU domain switching (SATP + SFENCE.VMA), not IOPMP (I/O Physical Memory Protection). The security spec targets IOPMP MDCFG tail-pointer bypasses via non-cascading hardware locks—a mechanism that does not exist in runtime code in this codebase (IOPMP is only mentioned in docs/domain_support.md as a conceptual future feature). The CPU-level PMP reconfiguration (lines 127-128) completes before the SATP switch, so domain protection is established before translations are changed. No MDCFG entries, tail pointers, or hardware-lock semantics are involved here.

